\begin{proposition}[$\signoise\to0$; separation threshold; sketched]\label{prop:scope-sigperp}
Assume (S1), (S2), (S4); $\Umat$ is the $\eta$-averaged second moment.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] As $\signoise\to0$: $\beta_t^2\to\Delta_t$, $\rho_\perp\to0$, $q\to\Delta_t+\dint(\alpha_t^2-\Delta_t)/\dlat$, all eigenvalues of $\hat\Sigma_{t,\perp}$ tend to $\Delta_t$ (no data-side broadening), and the noise-dim block sits on the diffusion floor
\begin{equation}\label{eq:scope-floor}
\lambda^{\rm nd}\to\frac{a_1(q)^2\Delta_t}{\dlat}\big(1\pm\psi_p^{-1/2}\big)^2,
\end{equation}
broadened only by $W$ (factor $1.65$ at $\psi_p=64$; measured $1.5$--$1.6$ at $\signoise=0.01$), with $N_{\rm nd}=D$. These modes carry no data variance, the learned null score equals the population one ($\varepsilon_\perp\to0$), and the data lie in the $\dint$-subspace, the setting of the linear pencil of \citet{george2025dsm} (\cref{rem:fourbulk-sigperp0}).
\item[(ii)] Including the data-side (ratio $\psi$) and width-side (ratio $D/\pwidth\approx\psi_p^{-1}$) Marchenko--Pastur broadenings, the lower edge of the noise-dim block is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:scope-ndmin}
\lambda^{\rm nd}_{\min}\asymp\frac{a_1(q)^2}{\dlat}\Big[e^{-2t}\signoise^2(1-\sqrt\psi)^2+\Delta_t\Big](1-\psi_p^{-1/2})^2\qquad(\psi<1),
\end{equation}
and with $G:=\log_{10}(\lambda^{\rm nd}_{\min}/\lambda_{\rm onset})$ the one-decade criterion $G\ge1$ of (S5) holds iff
\begin{equation}\label{eq:scope-sigthr}
\signoise^2\;\ge\;\frac{e^{2t}}{(1-\sqrt\psi)^2}\Big[\frac{10\,\dlat\,\lambda_{\rm onset}}{a_1(q)^2(1-\psi_p^{-1/2})^2}-\Delta_t\Big]=:(\signoise^*)^2 .
\end{equation}
\item[(iii)] With the measured onsets of the repo spectra ($t=0.01$, $\pwidth=64\dlat$, $\nsamp=500$, $\signoise=0.5$): $\signoise^*=0.91,0.70,0.50,0.45,0.41,0.40,0.42,0.52$ at $\dlat=10,20,40,60,80,100,150,200$. Hence $\signoise=0.5$ is below threshold for $\dlat\le20$ and at or above it for $\dlat\ge40$, matching the measured $G<1$ for $\dlat\le20$ and $G\ge1$ for $\dlat\ge30$ (measured $G=0.64,0.89,1.12,1.19,1.27,1.26,1.26,1.09$; predicted from \eqref{eq:scope-ndmin}: $0.51,0.73,0.99,1.08,1.14,1.15,1.11,0.98$, the product-of-edges lower edge under-estimating $\lambda^{\rm nd}_{\min}$ by $\approx25\%$). $\signoise=0.01$ lies below the floor ($\signoise^2=5\times10^{-3}\Delta_t$) and the measured separation there is $G=0.004$--$0.056$ decades: a three-bulk spectrum. The four-bulk picture applies only for $\signoise\gtrsim\signoise^*$.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(i) Substitute in \cref{def:scope-ratios} and \eqref{eq:scope-edges}; $\varepsilon_\perp^2\to0$ by \cref{prop:scope-nullspec}(iii); the null block of $\hat M_t$ is $e^{-2t}\hat C_\perp+\Delta_tI_D\to\Delta_tI_D$, so the only remaining broadening is that of $W_\perp^\top W_\perp/\pwidth$, with edges $(1\pm\sqrt{D/\pwidth})^2$. (ii) The non-zero eigenvalues of $a_1^2W_\perp\hat\Sigma_{t,\perp}W_\perp^\top/(\pwidth\dlat)$ equal those of $a_1^2\hat\Sigma_{t,\perp}^{1/2}(W_\perp^\top W_\perp/\pwidth)\hat\Sigma_{t,\perp}^{1/2}/\dlat$; the lower edges of the two factors are $e^{-2t}\signoise^2(1-\sqrt\psi)^2+\Delta_t$ and $(1-\sqrt{D/\pwidth})^2$, and their product is the lower edge of the free multiplicative convolution to leading order in the broadening (exact edges from the Silverstein equation, cf.\ \citealp{ELKaroui2010spectrum}). Solve $\lambda^{\rm nd}_{\min}\ge10\lambda_{\rm onset}$ for $\signoise^2$. (iii) Code units are $\pwidth\times$ theory units. $\dlat=10$: $\lambda_{\rm onset}=0.93/640=1.45\times10^{-3}$, $a_1(q)^2=0.281$, $\psi=0.01$, giving $(\signoise^*)^2=e^{0.02}(0.9)^{-2}[100\cdot1.45\times10^{-3}/(0.281\cdot0.766)-0.0198]=0.83$. $\dlat=40$: $\lambda_{\rm onset}=0.374/2560=1.46\times10^{-4}$, $a_1(q)^2=0.494$, $\psi=0.07$, giving $(\signoise^*)^2=1.0202\cdot1.849\cdot[400\cdot1.46\times10^{-4}/(0.494\cdot0.766)-0.0198]=0.256$, $\signoise^*=0.50$. The remaining entries are the same evaluation at the other $\dlat$. Measured $G$ uses only the noise-dim minimum and the sample-bulk top (index $\dlat+1$) of the released block summaries (whose block partition otherwise follows the superseded cumulative count): $\signoise=0.5$: $0.669,0.644,0.784,0.888,0.996,1.12,1.19,1.27,1.25,1.26,1.09$ at $\dlat=8,10,15,20,30,40,60,80,100,150,200$; $\signoise=0.01$: $0.045,0.032,0.019,0.006,0.004,0.056$ at $\dlat=8,\dots,40$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Four spectral blocks; sketched under Gaussian equivalence]\label{prop:fourblock-9}\label{prop:fourbulk-main}
Assume $\dint\ll\dlat<\nsamp<\pwidth$ with $\dint$ and $t>0$ fixed and $\dlat,\nsamp,\pwidth\to\infty$ at fixed $\gamma_\perp=(\dlat-\dint)/\nsamp$ and $\nsamp/\pwidth$; Gaussian equivalence of the feature Gram (\cref{ass:fourbulk-ge}); and $\signoise$ above the block-separation threshold $\signoise^*$ of \cref{prop:scope-sigperp} ($0.40$--$0.50$ for $\dlat\ge40$, satisfied at $\signoise=0.5$ and violated at $\signoise=0.01$). Then the sorted spectrum of $\Umat$ consists of four index blocks:
\begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{0pt}
\item[(i)] $\dint$ \emph{signal} outliers, population mean $a_1(q)^2\alpha_t^2/\dlat$, lifted by the null block as spiked-covariance perturbation theory prescribes \citep{baik2005phase};
\item[(ii)] $\dlat-\dint$ \emph{noise-dimension} modes of mean exactly $a_1(q)^2\beta_t^2/\dlat$, filling a Marchenko--Pastur band \citep{marchenko1967distribution} of support $\tfrac{a_1(q)^2}{\dlat}\bigl[e^{-2t}\signoise^2(1\pm\sqrt{\gamma_\perp})^2+\Delta_t\bigr]$;
\item[(iii)] $\nsamp-\dlat$ \emph{sample-specific} modes carrying the residual, nonlinear part of the features, mean $\simeq a_*(q)^2/\nsamp$ and top edge $\kappa_{\rm top}\,a_*(q)^2/\nsamp$;
\item[(iv)] $\pwidth-\nsamp$ \emph{residual-floor} modes, exact zeros for a single diffused copy.
\end{itemize}
Every data-side edge scales as $a_1(q(\dlat))^2/\dlat$ and is $\pwidth$-independent at leading order in $\dlat/\pwidth$; the sample block scales as $a_*(q(\dlat))^2/\nsamp$. The block means obey the exact trace identity
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fourbulk-trace-main}
\dint\frac{a_1^2\alpha_t^2}{\dlat}+(\dlat-\dint)\frac{a_1^2\beta_t^2}{\dlat}+\nsamp\frac{a_*^2}{\nsamp}=a_1(q)^2q+a_*(q)^2=\E\bigl[\tanh(\sqrt qz)^2\bigr],
\end{equation}
whose right side is $O(1)$ for every $\dlat$.
\end{proposition}
The counts follow from rank alone, by interlacing of a rank-$\dlat$ data term with the residual kernel (\cref{lem:fourbulk-counts,lem:fourbulk-bbp}, proved); the positions are sketched, and their drift with $\dlat$ through $q$ alone is reproduced with no fitted parameter in \cref{tab:fourbulk-numbers}, whereas a fixed-$a_1$ theory predicts flat blocks and is rejected. Blocks (i)--(ii) are disjoint iff $\lambda_{\min}(\hat C+\hat S_\parallel)>\signoise^2(1+\sqrt{\gamma_\perp})^2$, a condition in which $a_1^2$, $1/\dlat$ and $\pwidth$ cancel, and (ii)--(iii) are disjoint iff $\tfrac{\nsamp}{\dlat}\tfrac{a_1(q)^2}{a_*(q)^2}[e^{-2t}\signoise^2(1-\sqrt{\gamma_\perp})^2+\Delta_t]>\kappa_{\rm top}$ (\cref{app:theory-fourbulk}).\label{cor:fourbulk-separation-main} The measured spectra (\cref{fig:app-rfnn-exp2-pndr}) show the four blocks in every configuration, with the noise-dimension/sample gap at $0.6$--$1.3$ decades for $\signoise=0.5$ and closed at $\signoise=0.01$.

 egin{lemma}[Outliers of the spiked population covariance; proved]\label{lem:fourbulk-bbp}
Let $G=\Sigma^{1/2}(ZZ^	op/\pwidth)\Sigma^{1/2}$, $Z\in\R^{
samp	imes\pwidth}$ i.i.d.\ Gaussian, $\Sigma=D+\sigma_rI$ with $D\succeq0$ of rank $\dlat$ and eigenvalues $	heta_1\ge\dots\ge	heta_{\dlat}$ (homogeneous residual; for the heterogeneous residual replace the MP law by the MP--Silverstein image of $\operatorname{spec}(P_\perp K_{\mathrm{res}}P_\perp)/
samp$ and $\sigma_r$ in (b) by the mean residual eigenvalue). (a) For fixed $\dlat$ and $\gamma_p=
samp/\pwidth<1$, the eigenvalue of $G$ associated with $	heta_j$ is
 egin{equation}\label{eq:fourbulk-bbp}
  \lambda(	heta_j)=(	heta_j+\sigma_r)\Bigl(1+ rac{\gamma_p\sigma_r}{	heta_j}\Bigr)=	heta_j+\sigma_r(1+\gamma_p)+ rac{\gamma_p\sigma_r^2}{	heta_j}
  \qquad	ext{if}\qquad	heta_j>	heta_c=\sigma_r\sqrt{\gamma_p},
\end{equation}
and sticks to the edge $\sigma_r(1+\sqrt{\gamma_p})^2$ otherwise. (b) For any $\dlat<\min(
samp,\pwidth)$, conditionally on the $\dlat$ spiked rows $Z_1$ of $Z$, the restriction of $G$ to the complement of the spiked directions is $(\sigma_r/\pwidth)Z_2P^\perp Z_2^	op$, $P^\perp$ the projector off the row space of $Z_1$: a white Wishart with $\pwidth-\dlat$ samples in $
samp-\dlat$ dimensions, i.e.\ Marchenko--Pastur of ratio $(
samp-\dlat)/(\pwidth-\dlat)$, scale $\sigma_r(1-\dlat/\pwidth)$, expected mass $(
samp-\dlat)\sigma_r(1-\dlat/\pwidth)$ and top edge $\sigma_r\kappa_{\mathrm{MP}}$, with $\kappa_{\mathrm{MP}}=\pwidth^{-1}(\sqrt{
samp-\dlat}+\sqrt{\pwidth-\dlat})^2$ (the same formula holds for $\pwidth<
samp$, where the block has $\pwidth-\dlat$ modes). Since $\E\operatorname{tr}G=\operatorname{tr}\Sigma$, the $\dlat$ outliers carry expected mass $\sum_j	heta_j+\dlat\sigma_r(1+	frac{
samp-\dlat}{\pwidth})$: a mean shift of $\sigma_r(1+	frac{
samp-\dlat}{\pwidth})$ per outlier, consistent with \eqref{eq:fourbulk-bbp} at leading order in $\dlat/
samp$.
\end{lemma}
 egin{proof}
(a) is the spiked sample-covariance theorem \citep{baik2005phase,baik2006eigenvalues}, equivalently the multiplicative case of \citet{benaychgeorges2011eigenvalues}: a population eigenvalue $\ell=	heta+\sigma_r$ above $\sigma_r(1+\sqrt{\gamma_p})$ produces the outlier $\ell(1+\gamma_p\sigma_r/(\ell-\sigma_r))$. (b) In the spike eigenbasis $\Sigma^{1/2}Z=[(\Theta+\sigma_rI)^{1/2}Z_1;\ \sqrt{\sigma_r}Z_2]$; by interlacing for the $\pwidth	imes\pwidth$ dual $Z^	op\Sigma Z/\pwidth$, the non-spiked eigenvalues of $G$ are those of $\sigma_rZ_2P^\perp Z_2^	op/\pwidth$ up to $\dlat$ interlacing corrections, and $Z_2P^\perp$ has, given $Z_1$, i.i.d.\ Gaussian rows supported on a $(\pwidth-\dlat)$-dimensional subspace, which gives the Wishart statement, its mass $\sigma_r(
samp-\dlat)(\pwidth-\dlat)/\pwidth$ and its edge. Subtracting from $\operatorname{tr}\Sigma=\sum_j	heta_j+
samp\sigma_r$ gives the outlier mass.
\end{proof}
Numerical check of (b) (homogeneous, $
samp=500$, $\dlat=40$, $\pwidth=2560$, $5$ repetitions): bulk mass$/(\sigma_r(
samp-\dlat))=0.9840$ vs $1-\dlat/\pwidth=0.9844$; top edge$/\sigma_r=1.987$ vs $\kappa_{\mathrm{MP}}=2.005$, excluding the additive-model value $(1+\sqrt{\gamma_p})^2=2.079$. Individual outliers fluctuate by $	heta_j\sqrt{2/\pwidth}$, so the per-outlier shift ($\sigma_r$ vs $1.18\sigma_r$) is not resolvable in a single run; (b) is a statement in expectation. In code units the threshold is $	heta_c\pwidth\in[0.03,0.1]$ across the sweep while the smallest noise-dimension eigenvalue is $2$--$5$ at $\signoise=0.5$: every data direction is an outlier by a margin $\ge20	imes$, and the shift $\sigma_r$ is $0.15$--$0.25\%$ of $\lambda^{\mathrm{sig}}_{\min}$ ($a_*^2\pwidth/
samp=0.05$--$0.12$ vs $20$--$74$), which is the operational content of ``the data-side eigenvalues are $\pwidth$-independent'' beyond the $W$ factor of Step~3.

 egin{assumption}[Inputs to the buffer corollary; conjectured]\label{assump:inputs-buf}
 egin{itemize}
\item[(H1)] (\emph{Gaussian equivalence at block scale.}) With $a_1(q)$, $a_*(q)^2$ as before, write $\phi(x)=a_1(q)(\pwidth\dlat)^{-1/2}Wx+a_*(q)\pwidth^{-1/2}z(x)$, where $z(x)\in\R^{\pwidth}$ has zero mean, unit-variance coordinates, is uncorrelated with $Wx$, and has Gram $\E_W[z(x)^	op z(x')]/\pwidth=\kappa_{m res}(x\!\cdot\!x'/\dlat)/a_*(q)^2$,
 egin{equation}
\kappa_{m res}(ho)=\sum_{\ell\ {m odd}\ \ge3}c_\ell(q)^2\,(ho/q)^\ell,\qquad c_\ell(q):= rac{\E[	anh(\sqrt qz)\,\mathrm{He}_\ell(z)]}{\sqrt{\ell!}},\qquad\kappa_{m res}(q)=a_*(q)^2
\label{eq:kres-buf}
\end{equation}
(the Hermite series of \cref{lem:fourbulk-kernel}). We assume $\Umat=\Umat_{m lin}+\Umat_{m res}+E$ with $\Umat_{m lin}=a_1(q)^2(\dlat\pwidth)^{-1}WM_tW^	op$, $\Umat_{m res}=a_*(q)^2(
samp\pwidth)^{-1}\sum_\mu\E_\xi[z(x^\mu_t)z(x^\mu_t)^	op]$, and $E$ negligible \emph{at the scale of each block}: $\|P_{m data}EP_{m data}\|=o(a_1^2/\dlat)$, $\|P_{m samp}EP_{m samp}\|=o(	heta a_*^2/
samp)$. A plain $o(1)$ operator-norm statement would be vacuous (every block is $o(1)$). This is stronger than the ESD-level Gaussian-equivalence theorems \citep{goldt2022gaussian,hu2022universality,mei2022generalization}, is of the type proved for the extreme eigenvalues of the conjugate kernel with i.i.d.\ inputs by \citet{benigni2021eigenvalue}, and is assumed here for the $k$-centre mixture; its trace consequence is verified below.
\item[(H2)] (\emph{$\xi$-averaging.}) Two independent diffusion draws of the same $x^\mu$ have $\E[x^\mu_t\!\cdot\!x^{\mu\prime}_t]/\dlat=e^{-2t}\|x^\mu\|^2/\dlat=q-\Delta_t+o(1)$; hence the $\xi$-averaged residual feature $ ar z_\mu:=\E_\xi z(x^\mu_t)$ has coherent self-kernel $\kappa_{m res}(q-\Delta_t)=	heta(q)a_*(q)^2$, $	heta(q):=\kappa_{m res}(q-\Delta_t)/\kappa_{m res}(q)$ (\cref{lem:coeffs-buf}(v)). We assume the incoherent residual mass $(1-	heta)a_*^2$, spread over $\pwidth$ modes (exact $\E_\xi$) or $
samp(N_{m mc}-1)$ modes ($N_{m mc}$ draws), lies below the sample block: $(1-	heta)/\min(\pwidth,
samp N_{m mc})\ll	heta/
samp$. (On the repo's $N_{m mc}=50$ spectra $\lambda_{
samp+1}/\lambda_{
samp}=0.48$--$0.76$ for $\dlat\ge40$: the index-$
samp$ step is real but not sharp at finite $N_{m mc}$.)
\item[(H3)] (\emph{Asymptotic regime.}) $\dlat,
samp,\pwidth	o\infty$ with $\psi_n=
samp/\dlat$, $\psi_p=\pwidth/\dlat$, $q$ as parameters; along the sweep these move ($\psi_n$ from $100$ to $\approx1$), so no statement is uniform in $\dlat$, and finite-size corrections are $O(\dlat^{-1/2})$ relative ($\|x^\mu\|^2/\dlat$ fluctuates by $\approx25\%$ at $\dlat=5$: trace deficit $5\%$ there, $<1\%$ for $\dlat\ge100$). $\pwidth\ge
samp>\dlat$ is needed for the block counts; for $\pwidth=64\dlat$ it fails at $\dlat\le7$.
